#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# boolean_convert

#% text
# `boolean_convert(e, form)` writes the Boolean expression `e` in the given form.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `e` is an expression in symbols with `and`, `or`, `not`, `xor`, `nand`, `nor`, `implies`, `equivalent`, or a call of `boolean_function`; at most 14 variables.

#% item
# The form is a string in any case or a symbol: `"dnf"` (the default), `"cnf"`, `"anf"` or `"esop"` (exclusive or of products), `"nand"`, `"nor"`, `"binary_nand"`, `"binary_nor"` (only those operations, two arguments), `"ite"` (nested `if` on the variables in order), `"and"`, `"or"`, `"implies"` (written with `not`) and `"bff"` (a `boolean_function` call).

#% item
# `boolean_convert(e, form, cond)` or `boolean_convert(e, cond)` need agree with `e` only where `cond` is true.

#% item
# The sum of products is the Quine-McCluskey prime implicants with the essential ones and then a greedy cover: small, not always the smallest.

#% item
# A function of the wrong kind gives `failure(argument, boolean_convert, k)`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
boolean_convert(equivalent(x, y, z))
#% output
#= x and y and z or not x and not y and not z

#% text
# Several forms of an exclusive or:

#% cell
map(f => boolean_convert(xor(x, y), f), ["dnf", "cnf", "nand", "nor"])
#% output
#= [x and not y or not x and y, (not x or not y) and (x or y), nand(nand(x, not y), nand(not x, y)), nor(nor(not x, not y), nor(x, y))]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Under a condition:

#% cell
boolean_convert(xor(x, y, z), "dnf", x and (y or z))
#% output
#= y and z

#% text
# The algebraic normal form:

#% cell
boolean_convert(implies(a, b), "anf")
#% output
#= xor(true, a, a and b)

#% text
# A check of equivalence:

#% cell
e = boolean_counting_function([2, 3], 4)(a, b, c, d)
is_tautology(equivalent(e, boolean_convert(e, "cnf"), boolean_convert(e, "nand")))
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The exclusive-or form and the `ite` form of an implication, which is equivalent to it:

#% cell
[boolean_convert(a and b, "esop"), boolean_convert(implies(a, b), "ite"), is_tautology(equivalent(implies(a, b), boolean_convert(implies(a, b), "ite")))]
#% output
#= [a and b, if a then if b then true else false else true, true]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# An unknown form, and an expression that is not Boolean, give a failure that names the argument:

#% cell
[boolean_convert(a or b, "foo"), boolean_convert(1)]
#% output
#= [failure(argument, boolean_convert, 2), failure(argument, boolean_convert, 1)]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `boolean_minimize`, `boolean_function`, `logical_expand`, `is_tautology`
